The Central Limit Theorem tells us that for a sufficiently large sample size (like 36 in this case), the distribution of the sample mean ($\bar{X}$) will be approximately normally distributed, regardless of the original distribution. The mean of this distribution will be equal to the population mean (which is 3 in this case) and the standard deviation will be equal to the population standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size.

Given an exponential distribution, the standard deviation is equal to the mean (which is 3). Therefore, the standard deviation of the sample mean distribution is $3/\sqrt{36}=0.5$.

We want to find $P(2.5 \leq \bar{X} \leq 4)$. To do this, we can standardize these values and use the standard normal distribution (mean 0, standard deviation 1). The standardized values will be:

$$
Z_1 = \frac{2.5 - 3}{0.5} = -1
$$

and

$$
Z_2 = \frac{4 - 3}{0.5} = 2
$$

We find the probabilities associated with these Z-scores from the standard normal distribution table:

$$
P(-1 \leq Z \leq 2) = P(Z \leq 2) - P(Z \leq -1) \approx 0.8413 - 0.1587 = 0.6826
$$

Unfortunately, none of the given options match this result. There might be a mistake in the options or in the interpretation of the question. However, based on the given information and calculations, the probability is approximately 0.6826.
